        </div>

       <div class="ch">
<h1>Astable multivibrator with a 555 timer</h1>

<div class="ch-card">
<h2>How it works</h2>
<ul>
<li>The capacitor <strong>C1</strong> charges through <strong>R1 + R2</strong> toward Vcc until its voltage reaches <strong>2/3 Vcc</strong> (the upper comparator, pin <code>thres</code>): the output goes low and the discharge pin <code>disch</code> connects to ground.</li>
<li>Now C1 discharges through <strong>R2</strong> alone until it falls to <strong>1/3 Vcc</strong> (the lower comparator, pin <code>trig</code>): the output goes high again and the cycle repeats.</li>
<li><code>trig</code> and <code>thres</code> are tied together to the capacitor. <code>reset</code> is tied to Vcc (not used) and <strong>C2</strong> (10 nF) decouples the control pin <code>ctrl</code>. The output <code>out</code> is left open.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Formulas</h2>
<ul>
<li>High time: <code>t<sub>H</sub> = 0.693 · (R1 + R2) · C1</code> = 0.693 · 11 kΩ · 100 nF ≈ 0.76 ms</li>
<li>Low time: <code>t<sub>L</sub> = 0.693 · R2 · C1</code> = 0.693 · 10 kΩ · 100 nF ≈ 0.69 ms</li>
<li>Frequency: <code>f = 1.44 / ((R1 + 2 · R2) · C1)</code> ≈ 686 Hz, duty cycle ≈ 52 %</li>
</ul>

<h2>Circuit</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>V1:</strong> 5 V supply. <strong>U1:</strong> model <code>Timer555</code> (behavioural NE555: output high = Vcc − 1.5 V, low = 0.1 V).</li>
<li><strong>R1</strong> = 1 kΩ, <strong>R2</strong> = 10 kΩ, <strong>C1</strong> = 100 nF, <strong>C2</strong> = 10 nF.</li>
<li>Time domain analysis: 0 ... 8 ms with a step of 2 µs (the model changes state only at the accepted time points, so keep the step well below the pulse width). Plotted: <code>out</code> and the capacitor voltage <code>thr</code>.</li>
</ul>
</div>

    </div>
    </div></foreignObject></g>